from a_common import P,pt,table,svg,txt,line,box

def build(B):
    c='A4.1'
    B.add(c,'sl','Recognizing an evolutionary change','Which observation is sufficient evidence of an evolutionary change in a population?',correct='An inherited variant becomes more frequent across generations.',wrong=['Every individual becomes temporarily dehydrated during one afternoon.','One animal learns a route to food during its lifetime.','The same plants grow taller after receiving more water, without evidence of inherited change.'],why='Evolution involves change in heritable characteristics of a population across generations; individual environmental responses or learning alone do not establish this.',statements='A4.1.1')
    B.add(c,'sl','Inherited change or environmental response','A constructed study compares two plant populations grown in their native habitats. Population P has larger leaves. When offspring from both populations are grown under the same controlled conditions, their leaf-size distributions overlap almost completely.',[P('Explain why the field difference alone does not demonstrate evolution.','Leaf size may differ because of environmental conditions rather than an inherited difference between populations.'),P('Explain how the offspring experiment changes the interpretation.','A common environment removes some environmental differences; the overlap weakens the evidence that the field contrast is inherited.'),P('State one limitation of concluding that the populations are genetically identical.','One measured trait under one set of conditions does not test all genes or all possible inherited differences.')],data=True,statements='A4.1.1')
    B.add(c,'sl','Comparing molecular evidence','Constructed alignments compare the same 300-base DNA region in three mammal species. Species P and Q differ at 12 positions, P and R at 39, and Q and R at 36.',[P('Calculate the percentage sequence identity of P and Q.',r'$288/300\times100=96.0\%$.'),P('Deduce which pair is most closely related according to these data.','P and Q, because their aligned region has the fewest differences/highest identity.'),P('Explain why several independent regions would strengthen the inference.','A single gene may have an unusual evolutionary history or rate; agreement across independent regions provides stronger evidence of common ancestry.')],data=True,statements='A4.1.2')
    B.add(c,'sl','Selection in a domesticated crop',r'In a constructed breeding programme, only plants with the highest seed mass are used as parents. Mean seed mass rises from $2.4\,\mathrm g$ to $3.0\,\mathrm g$ over several generations in the same growing conditions.',[P('Calculate the percentage increase in mean seed mass.',r'$0.6/2.4\times100=25\%$.'),P('Explain why the procedure is artificial selection.','Humans choose parents according to a desired characteristic.','If variation in seed mass is heritable, selected parents pass relevant variants to offspring, changing the population across generations.'),P('State one reason the result does not mean every future seed must have a mass of 3.0 g.','A mean allows individual variation, and both inherited differences and environmental effects can influence individual seed mass.')],data=True,statements='A4.1.3')
    limb=svg(txt(410,28,'Simplified forelimb organization; sizes are not comparable')+txt(210,68,'Forelimb P')+txt(610,68,'Forelimb Q')+
        ''.join(line(cx,95,cx,155,width=16)+line(cx-15,170,cx-23,230,width=9)+line(cx+15,170,cx+23,230,width=9)+
        ''.join(line(cx+(j-2)*11,250,cx+(j-2)*28,310,width=5) for j in range(5)) for cx in [210,610])+
        txt(410,125,'one proximal bone',15)+txt(410,205,'two distal bones',15)+txt(410,290,'five digit rays',15),h=360)
    B.add(c,'sl','A shared limb pattern','The schematic summarizes the same underlying forelimb arrangement in two mammals. P uses its forelimb mainly for swimming and Q mainly for grasping.',[P('Explain why the limbs are homologous structures.','They share an underlying structural pattern inherited from a common ancestor, despite different functions.'),P('Explain how their differences can arise while homology is retained.','Evolution can modify the size, shape or proportions of corresponding bones for different functions without removing their common origin.'),P('State one additional type of evidence that could test the proposed common ancestry.','Compare DNA/protein sequences, development or fossils; credit a relevant independent source.')],diagram=limb,statements='A4.1.4')
    B.add(c,'sl','Similar function with a different origin','Insect wings are outgrowths of the exoskeleton; bird wings are modified vertebrate forelimbs. Both can provide powered flight. Which conclusion best describes this comparison?',correct='Their flight surfaces are analogous: a similar function evolved independently in these lineages.',wrong=['The two flight surfaces must have evolved once in the most recent common ancestor of insects and birds.','Similar function proves that the underlying structures are homologous.','A shared ability to fly means insects and birds belong to the same species.'],why='Similar selective pressures can produce convergent functional solutions from different ancestral structures.',statements='A4.1.5')
    B.add(c,'sl','Net change in a lineage’s species richness','A constructed island lineage initially contains 8 species. Over a long interval, 5 speciation events each add one species and 3 species become extinct. Assume no immigration or emigration.',[P('Calculate the final number of species.','$8+5-3=10$ species.'),P('Explain why speciation and extinction must both be considered when interpreting diversity.','Speciation adds lineages while extinction removes them; their combined balance determines net change in species richness.'),P('Explain why the total alone cannot reveal which species survived throughout the interval.','Different histories of origin and loss can produce the same final count; lineage-specific evidence is required.')],data=True,statements='A4.1.6')
    B.add(c,'sl','A river and separated primate populations','A hypothesis links divergence of the lineages leading to bonobos and chimpanzees to geographical separation associated with the Congo River.',[P('Explain how a persistent river barrier can promote divergence.','The barrier can reduce interbreeding/gene flow between populations.','Separated populations can accumulate different heritable changes under different selection pressures or by chance.'),P('Explain why a barrier’s presence alone does not prove that it caused speciation.','The timing of the barrier and lineage divergence must agree, and alternative causes or later contact must be considered.')],statements='A4.1.7')
    B.add(c,'sl','Testing a geographical barrier','A constructed study compares fish in tributaries connected by open water with fish separated by a waterfall. Genetic differences are greater across the waterfall, but the separated sites also differ in temperature.',[P('Suggest how the waterfall could contribute to genetic divergence.','It may restrict movement and interbreeding, reducing gene flow.'),P('Explain why temperature is relevant to interpreting the result.','Different temperatures could favour different inherited variants, so divergence cannot be assigned solely to reduced movement.'),P('Propose one improvement to the study.','Sample several matched connected and separated site pairs with similar temperatures, or measure movement and gene flow directly; credit a suitable design.')],data=True,statements='A4.1.7')
    B.add(c,'sl','Independent evidence for common ancestry','A constructed comparison groups species X and Y together using both their corresponding limb bones and several independent DNA regions. Species Z is more different by both measures.',[P('Explain why agreement between the two evidence types is useful.','Independent anatomical and molecular observations support the same common-ancestry interpretation.','Agreement makes an explanation based only on superficial functional similarity or an unusual single gene less persuasive.'),P('Explain why this conclusion does not require X to be the ancestor of Y.','X and Y can be separate descendant lineages sharing a more recent common ancestor.')],data=True,statements='A4.1.2, A4.1.4')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Speciation within the same region','Two insect populations occupy the same geographical region but mate on different host plants and rarely exchange genes. Which description is most appropriate for a proposed speciation process under these conditions?',correct='Sympatric speciation associated with reproductive separation within the same geographical area.',wrong=['Allopatric speciation necessarily caused by a physical barrier between the regions.','Convergent evolution that requires the populations to have unrelated ancestors.','Artificial selection because the populations use different plants.'],why='Sympatric speciation occurs without geographical separation; ecological or behavioural differences can reduce interbreeding within one area.',statements='A4.1.8')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Seasonal separation of flowering','Constructed observations show that plant population P flowers mainly in weeks 1–3 and population Q in weeks 6–8. Their pollen can fertilize each other in hand-pollination trials and produce fertile offspring.',[P('Identify the reproductive barrier suggested in nature.','Temporal isolation: flowering occurs at different times.'),P('Explain why the hand-pollination result does not remove the natural barrier.','Experimental pollen transfer bypasses the normal lack of overlap between reproductive periods.'),P('Predict how a change causing flowering periods to overlap might affect gene flow, with a qualification.','Gene flow could increase if pollinators transfer pollen between the populations; other barriers may still prevent interbreeding.')],data=True,statements='A4.1.8')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Testing adaptive radiation','A constructed island study finds five closely related bird species occupying different feeding niches. They share a recent common ancestor according to several DNA regions, and their beak forms correlate with different food types.',[P('Explain how these observations support adaptive radiation.','A single ancestral lineage has diversified into several descendant species.','The descendant forms have adaptations associated with different ecological niches.'),P('Explain why observing different beak shapes alone would be insufficient.','Different shapes do not by themselves establish common ancestry, species formation or adaptive differences in feeding success.')],data=True,statements='A4.1.9')
    B.add(c,'ahl','A viable but usually sterile hybrid','A horse gamete has 32 chromosomes and a donkey gamete has 31. Their hybrid offspring is usually sterile.',[P('Calculate the chromosome number of the hybrid.','$32+31=63$.'),P('Explain why the offspring can grow normally yet have difficulty producing functional gametes.','Many body-cell processes can function with the mixed chromosome set.','During meiosis, some chromosomes lack suitable homologous partners, causing irregular segregation and unbalanced gametes.'),P('Identify whether the fertility barrier is prezygotic or postzygotic.','Postzygotic: fertilization and hybrid development occur before the fertility problem.')],statements='A4.1.10')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Chromosome doubling in a plant hybrid','In a constructed example, two plant species each produce gametes containing 10 chromosomes. Their hybrid has one set from each species and is sterile because the sets cannot pair effectively. Chromosome doubling occurs in a cell that forms a new reproductive shoot.',[P('State the chromosome number before and after doubling.','Before: 20; after: 40.'),P('Explain how doubling could restore fertility.','Each chromosome now has a homologous partner derived from the same parental species.','More regular pairing and segregation can produce balanced gametes.'),P('Explain how the fertile doubled lineage could become reproductively separated from its parents.','Crosses with the original parental ploidy levels can produce unbalanced chromosome sets and reduced fertility, limiting gene flow.')],data=True,statements='A4.1.11')
    B.add(c,'ahl','Courtship as a reproductive barrier','In a constructed playback experiment, females from population P approach the song of P males in 80% of trials and the song of Q males in 10%. Both populations occur at the same site.',[P('Explain how the result could contribute to reproductive isolation.','A strong preference for the local song can reduce mating between populations even when they meet.'),P('Explain why approach responses alone do not demonstrate complete speciation.','Approach is not a direct measure of mating, fertilization or fertile offspring, and some cross-population responses occur.'),P('Propose one control that improves the experiment.','Standardize playback loudness, distance, duration and testing conditions; randomize song order or include multiple male recordings to avoid recording-specific effects. Credit one justified control.')],data=True,statements='A4.1.8')

    c='A4.2'
    B.add(c,'sl','Recognizing levels of biodiversity','A conservation programme protects different wetlands, many bird species and inherited variation within a rare reed population. Which mapping is correct?',correct='Wetland types: ecosystem diversity; bird species: species diversity; inherited reed variation: genetic diversity.',wrong=['Wetland types: genetic diversity; bird species: ecosystem diversity; inherited reed variation: species diversity.','All three examples measure species richness only.','Inherited variation within the reed population is ecosystem diversity.'],why='Biodiversity includes variation within species, among species and among ecosystems.',statements='A4.2.1')
    B.add(c,'sl','Richness and evenness','Two constructed samples each contain 40 beetles from four species.'+table(['Sample','Species P','Species Q','Species R','Species S'],[['A',10,10,10,10],['B',34,2,2,2]]),[P('Compare species richness.','Both samples contain four species, so richness is equal.'),P('Compare evenness.','A has greater evenness because its individuals are equally distributed among the species, whereas B is dominated by P.'),P('Explain why total abundance alone would miss this difference.','The totals are equal but do not describe how individuals are distributed among species.')],data=True,statements='A4.2.1, A4.2.5')
    B.add(c,'sl','Interpreting a repeated biodiversity survey','Constructed surveys use the same traps, locations, season and total trap-hours. Mean moth abundance falls from 150 per survey to 90 over ten years, while recorded species richness falls from 24 to 18.',[P('Calculate the percentage decline in abundance.',r'$60/150\times100=40\%$.'),P('Explain why standardizing sampling effort matters.','It reduces the possibility that the apparent decline is simply caused by fewer traps or less observation time.'),P('State one limitation of attributing the change to a particular pollutant.','The observations show a decline but do not isolate its cause; habitat, weather, other chemicals or detection changes could contribute.')],data=True,statements='A4.2.2, A4.2.5')
    B.add(c,'sl','Human pressure and an extinct moa','The North Island giant moa, *Dinornis novaezealandiae*, is extinct. Archaeological evidence of moa remains in human food-processing sites supports hunting as a major pressure; habitat modification also affected New Zealand’s ecosystems.',[P('Explain how sustained hunting can cause extinction.','If human-caused mortality exceeds replacement by reproduction for long enough, population size falls.','When all remaining populations disappear, the species becomes extinct.'),P('Explain why evidence for hunting does not establish that it was the only pressure.','Multiple pressures can act together; hunting evidence does not exclude habitat change or other contributors.')],statements='A4.2.3',notes='Historical moa example uses the corrected guide species and general archaeological hunting evidence; no fabricated measurements or exact extinction date.')
    B.add(c,'sl','Interacting pressures on Caribbean monk seals','Historical accounts reviewed by NOAA describe exploitation of Caribbean monk seals for oil and meat, killing by fishers and human disturbance at coastal resting sites. The species is now extinct.',[P('Explain one direct route by which exploitation reduced the population.','Killing removed breeding or potentially breeding individuals and could exceed replacement through reproduction.'),P('Explain how coastal disturbance could reinforce the impact of exploitation.','Loss or avoidance of resting/breeding sites can reduce suitable habitat or reproductive success, adding pressure to an already depleted population.'),P('Explain why successful protection would have needed to act before the final individuals disappeared.','Protection can allow surviving populations to recover, but cannot recreate an extinct species once no individuals remain.')],statements='A4.2.3',notes='Historical facts paraphrased from NOAA Caribbean Monk Seal 5-Year Review (2008), https://repository.library.noaa.gov/view/noaa/17045/noaa_17045_DS1.pdf; no claimed live observations or copied questions.')
    B.add(c,'sl','Using a third extinction case','The thylacine was an Australian marsupial. Historical records document extensive hunting, including bounty payments; habitat change and disease have also been implicated in its decline. The last known captive individual died in 1936.',[P('Explain how bounty payments could increase extinction risk.','They created an incentive to kill more animals, increasing mortality in a declining population.'),P('Explain why the date of the last known captive death is not necessarily the exact date the last wild animal died.','Wild individuals may not have been observed, so records of the last known captive individual do not establish the final wild death.'),P('Suggest a general conservation lesson from this case.','Reduce identified pressures and protect viable populations early, before numbers become too small for recovery; credit a justified lesson.')],statements='A4.2.3',notes='Factual context checked against National Museum of Australia: https://www.nma.gov.au/defining-moments/resources/extinction-of-thylacine. Exact final wild extinction timing is deliberately not asserted.')
    B.add(c,'sl','Conversion of dipterocarp forest',r'A constructed land-use example models conversion of Southeast Asian dipterocarp forest to a single-crop plantation. Forest area falls from $1200\,\mathrm{km^2}$ to $780\,\mathrm{km^2}$.', [P('Calculate the percentage loss of forest area.',r'$420/1200\times100=35\%$.'),P('Explain why replacing many forest plant species with one crop can reduce biodiversity even if tree cover remains.','Species diversity and structural/habitat variety can decline, reducing niches and resources for other organisms.'),P('Explain why protecting only the same total number of trees would be an incomplete conservation target.','Tree count does not capture native species composition, age structure, connectivity or associated ecological communities.')],data=True,statements='A4.2.4')
    B.add(c,'sl','Wetland loss and ecosystem diversity','A constructed regional assessment records six distinct wetland types initially. Drainage eliminates all examples of two types, while some areas of the other four remain.',[P('Distinguish loss of wetland area from loss of ecosystem diversity in this case.','Area measures how much wetland remains; ecosystem diversity concerns the variety of wetland types.','Eliminating two entire types reduces that variety from six to four even if large areas of the other types survive.'),P('Explain why preserving only the most common type would be insufficient.','Organisms and ecological functions associated with rarer types may be lost even if the common type is well protected.')],data=True,statements='A4.2.1, A4.2.4')
    B.add(c,'sl','Citizen-science records and sampling bias','In a constructed citizen-science project, observers report far more bird records near roads than in remote forest. Search effort is not recorded.',[P('Explain why record counts cannot be directly interpreted as relative bird abundance.','More people may visit accessible roadside sites, so greater observation effort can produce more records without more birds.'),P('Suggest two improvements that retain public participation.','Record observation duration, route length or number of observers to account for effort.','Use standardized survey locations/times or recruit coverage of under-sampled sites; credit a second valid improvement.'),P('State one benefit of citizen science when data quality is managed.','Many participants can provide broad geographical or repeated temporal coverage that would otherwise be costly.')],data=True,statements='A4.2.5')
    B.add(c,'sl','Evolutionary distinctiveness in conservation','Two threatened mammal species have similar extinction risk. Species P is the only surviving member of a deeply separated lineage, while Q has several close living relatives.',[P('Explain why P may receive high priority under an EDGE approach.','EDGE considers both evolutionary distinctiveness and global endangerment.','Loss of P would remove a greater amount of unique evolutionary history than loss of one species with several very close relatives, given the stated comparison.'),P('Explain why this does not mean Q has no conservation value.','Q still contributes biological diversity and ecological or cultural value; priority frameworks guide limited resources rather than assigning zero value to other species.')],statements='A4.2.8')
    B.add(c,'sl','Combining conservation methods','A rare plant is protected in its native habitat while seeds from many unrelated plants are stored under controlled conditions. Which interpretation is most accurate?',correct='Habitat protection is in situ conservation; seed storage is ex situ conservation and can provide a backup genetic resource.',wrong=['Seed storage alone preserves every ecological interaction of the natural population.','Both methods are ex situ because humans manage them.','Protecting the habitat is ex situ, while storage away from the habitat is in situ.'],why='In situ acts within the natural habitat; ex situ conserves material outside it. They can complement each other but do not preserve identical processes.',statements='A4.2.6-7')
    B.add(c,'sl','Monitoring a seed-bank collection','Constructed tests show that 184 of 200 stored seeds germinate initially and 138 of 200 germinate after several years under the same test conditions.',[P('Calculate germination percentage in each test.',r'Initially $184/200\times100=92\%$; later $138/200\times100=69\%$.'),P('Calculate the decrease in percentage points.','$92-69=23$ percentage points.'),P('Explain one action the results could justify.','Renew the collection or adjust validated storage conditions before viability declines further, while maintaining genetic representation.'),P('Explain why collecting seeds from only one parent would be a limitation.','The collection would represent only a small part of the population’s genetic diversity.')],data=True,statements='A4.2.1, A4.2.7')
    B.add(c,'sl','Evaluating a habitat corridor','A proposed vegetated corridor would connect two protected forest patches. A rare mammal is reluctant to cross open farmland, but an invasive predator also moves through dense vegetation.',[P('Explain a potential conservation benefit.','The corridor may allow the rare mammal to disperse and interbreed between patches, improving access to habitat and genetic exchange.'),P('Explain a potential cost.','It may also assist spread of the invasive predator, increasing mortality of the rare mammal.'),P('Propose evidence needed to judge whether to build or modify the corridor.','Measure movement and survival of both species, and test corridor designs or predator control that retain connectivity while limiting harm.')],statements='A4.2.6')
    B.add(c,'sl','Testing invasive-species removal','In a constructed replicated study, native seedling density changes from 12 to 28 per plot after invasive plants are removed. Matched untreated plots change from 13 to 15 over the same interval.',[P('Calculate the change in each treatment.','Removal plots: $28-12=16$; untreated plots: $15-13=2$ seedlings per plot.'),P('Explain what the untreated plots contribute to the interpretation.','They estimate background change over the same period, so the larger increase following removal is less easily explained by a shared seasonal effect alone.'),P('State one design feature needed for a strong causal interpretation.','Randomly allocate comparable plots, replicate treatments and standardize measurements; credit one relevant feature.'),P('Explain why measuring one season alone may be insufficient for conservation decisions.','Effects may change with regrowth, climate or delayed ecological responses, so long-term monitoring is needed.')],data=True,statements='A4.2.5-6')
    B.add(c,'sl','Choosing a complementary set of reserves','A constructed plan can protect two equally sized sites. Site P supports species 1, 2, 3 and 4; site Q supports 1, 2, 3 and 5; site R supports 5, 6 and 7. Costs and predicted persistence are assumed equal.',[P('Determine which two-site choice maximizes the number of distinct species represented.','P and R represent seven species (1–7); P and Q represent five; Q and R represent six.'),P('Explain why choosing sites only by individual species richness would fail here.','The two individually richest sites P and Q have substantial overlap; R complements P with more additional species.'),P('State one biological factor omitted from the simplified decision.','Population viability, habitat quality, connectivity, genetic diversity, ecosystem representation or future threats; credit one relevant factor.')],data=True,statements='A4.2.1, A4.2.6')
    B.add(c,'sl','Absence of records and extinction evidence','A constructed survey fails to detect a rare nocturnal mammal in ten daytime visits to a small part of its former range.',[P('Explain why the result is weak evidence of extinction.','The species may be active at night or present outside the surveyed area, so detection probability is low.'),P('Suggest two changes that would make a future assessment more informative.','Survey at appropriate times using validated methods such as night cameras or indirect signs.','Increase geographical and temporal coverage with repeated standardized sampling.'),P('Explain why precautionary protection may still be justified during further surveys.','Failure to detect the species is uncertain; if survivors remain, protecting habitat can prevent further loss while evidence is gathered.')],data=True,statements='A4.2.2, A4.2.5-6')
